of course there are lots of scenes in  the harry potter books that didn't make  it into the films  but did you know that there are also  plenty of moments in the movies that jk  rowling didn't actually write  discover which movie moments were  created by the filmmakers  from important interactions between  characters to funny lines that provide  some much needed light relief  there's a whole scene that inspired a  lego set and there's even an entire  character that didn't exist in the  novels  in the deathly hallows part one harry  and hermione take a moment to break the  tension during their long tense horcrux  hunt  while nick cave's oh children plays in  the background harry pulls hermione up  for a dance  she's reluctant at first but then starts  to smile along with harry as they twirl  each other around  it's an important scene as it explores  their platonic friendship  there's no hint of romance in it just  two close buddies supporting one another  if only ron could have seen them he'd  know there was no need to worry  something was going on between them  sorry harry and hermione shippers i saw  you two the other night  brothers that's nothing in the books  molly weasley made it clear that it  wasn't okay for sirius to treat harry as  if he was his best friend  james but the order of the phoenix movie  takes that even further  making it explicit that sirius mistook  harry for james in the department of  mysteries  the look on harry's face sums up the  devastation he feels  realizing that his godfather's  connection to him was mainly due to his  connection with harry's late father  it harks back to the flashback scene in  snape's worst memory too  when james uses expelliarmus and sirius  says the exact same phrase  there's another dancing scene that  wasn't in the books but this one is  lighthearted and funny  in the goblet of fire movie professor  mcgonagall teaches students how to dance  in preparation for the yule ball the  teacher wasn't so excited about the  upcoming party in the book  but in the film she was much more into  it getting ron up in front of everyone  else to show them how it's done  poor ron is mortified much to the  amusement of fred  george and harry never gonna let him  forget this  are you never the scene in the  half-blood prince movie when bellatrix  lestrange and fenrir greyback attack the  weasley's house is so iconic  that lego made a whole set based on it  but it's not  actually in the book so why did the film  producers create this devastating moment  well there's a fan theory that makes a  lot of sense firstly  it moves harry and ginny's relationship  forward with the pair's bond growing  it provides action to the middle section  of the movie which otherwise would have  been devoid of any major moment  and we also see lupine and tonks as a  couple plus it gives helena bonham  carter  more air time  we get a lot of details in the half  blood prince book about dumbledore's  passing and his funeral  but the movie provides an extra moment  of mourning that really moves us  after harry goes over to the body and  the students and staff all watch on in  utter sadness  professor mcgonagall raises her wand to  the sky the rest of the mourners follow  her lead and we see a sea of wands held  aloft to honor their late headmaster  the overhead view is stunning with all  the lit tips of the wands  shining upwards and the accompanying  instrumental music  perfectly matches the melancholy scene  [Music]  in the opening of half-blood prince film  harry is reading the daily prophet in a  cafe  he flirts with a waitress and when she  turns her back he hilariously checks his  breath and pops a mint in his mouth  they even plan a date but dumbledore  turns up and that's the end of that  it's a fun scene that shows harry acting  like a regular person who isn't always  preoccupied with saving the world  it replaces the book scene where harry's  reading the paper at the dursleys and  dumbledore turns up at four privet drive  we would have loved to see that happen  too she was truthfully  very pretty the girl one of harry's most  endearing features is his ability  to crack an unexpected joke and he does  this in a scene during the half-blood  prince when hermione is trying to help  him figure out who to take to slughorn's  christmas party  she tells him that ramilda vayne trying  to smuggle you a love potion  harry's funny reply shows a sense of  humor and that he can have light-hearted  moments about being a reluctant  celebrity  she's only interested in you because she  thinks you're the chosen one  this moment in the half-blood prince  isn't an entire scene that's unique to  the film  but rather a few funny sections that  didn't appear in the book  perry taking professor slughorn down to  hagrid's for aragog's funeral features  in both the book  and the movie but dan radcliffe's  impression of a giant spider with  pincers  is particular to the film and it's a  wonderful bit of light relief  in the deathly hallows part 2 film we  get an extra draco moment that jk  rowling didn't create  but which shows how he's torn between  loyalty to his family  and his deep down desire to be a better  guy when his parents summon him over to  their side after the battle of hogwarts  something seriously creepy happens lord  voldemort reaches out and gives draco  the most awkward hug  ever well done  the looks on the faces of the students  watching the disturbing scene  sum up how creeped out we feel too jk  rowling gives harry  and us readers some much welcome  information about harry's mom  through slughorn's memories of lily  these translate from the book to the  film but we also get an extra insight  into lily's personality in the  half-blood prince movie  that rowling didn't include in the book  slughorn reveals that lily made him a  pet fish  which she created out of a flower petal  and the fish vanished when she passed  away  it's a touching moment which provides a  greater insight into just how kind lily  was  our battle had come from a lily  your mother during the battle of  hogwarts in the deathly hallows part 2  neville has a heroic moment where he  leads the snatchers across the covered  wooden bridge  in the book it's mentioned that neville  dueled scabior  and some snatchers met their fate by  falling from the bridge  but in the film adaptation it's  elaborated we see professor mcgonagall  telling neville and sheamus to use  pyrotechnics to rig the bridge  and then we see the visually impressive  moment when the bridge collapses  it might not have been in jk rowling's  original vision but we're in favor of  neville having more chances to shine  that went well there's a whole character  who appears in the movies that doesn't  even exist in the books  and that's nigel wolpert a gryffindor we  first meet in the goblet of fire film  he becomes a member of dumbledore's army  and has that pollen creevy style  admiration of harry  and the squad in fact it's thought that  nigel was a replacement for colin who  continued to feature in the books until  the end  but only appeared in the chamber of  secrets movie nigel popped up in various  scenes  always eager to help he even delivered  ron's mail hoping that he'd get harry's  autograph in return  bless  there's another moment in the deathly  hallows part 2 that's particular to the  movie  when snape calls an assembly after  hearing that harry was spotted in  hogsmeade  harry doesn't stay under the  invisibility cloak in front of the whole  school  can confront snape about his role in  dumbledore's passing  mcgonagall and snape's stool follows  before cerberus escapes  how dare you stand where he stood in the  deathly hallows book hermione reveals to  harry that she used the obliviate spell  to wipe herself from her parents  memories  but in the movie we actually get to  watch the moment it happens  it's an emotional scene and helps the  audience connect much more deeply with  hermione's pain  than if we had simply watched her tell  harry about it  as with many of these scenes it can be  presented differently on screen because  the films aren't so closely tied to only  showing what harry is experiencing  that allows us viewers to develop  greater empathy with other characters  the movies charted hermione and ron's  changing relationship really well  but they took a few liberties with the  source material along the way  like in the chamber of secrets film when  hermione joined her friends again after  being cured from being petrified  she rushes up to harry and gives him a  big hug and then she turns to ron  they both lean in for a hug but instead  they settle for an awkward handshake  it's a perfect way to sum up their  complicated relationship but it didn't  actually happen in the book that way  which of these scenes do you think earns  its place in the movies the most  and which one do you think isn't worthy  of messing with jk's original vision  let us know in the comments section and  give this video a like if you love the  wizarding world  until next time potterheads thanks for  watching  [Music]
 

